{"message": "Drafting ACIA-POL-2026-117 now. Two things before the package goes to the President & CEO:

1. Briefing gap worth your call. The staff summary (updated 2 Oct) says GAAIA is "not yet formally introduced," but Obernolte and Trahan introduced the frontier-oversight core of the draft as a standalone bill on 23 July: H.R. 9925, the FRONTIER Act (74 pages; definitions, frontier AI frameworks, IVO audits, incident reporting, preemption). The full GAAIA is still a discussion draft and GAAIA@mail.house.gov is still the right channel. Default plan: keep the letter addressed to the discussion draft, with one short paragraph noting our comments apply with equal force to H.R. 9925. Say the word if you want that struck.

2. Section numbers resolved. The summary's TODO is done. I checked the sponsors' section-by-section and text: definitions Sec. 101; CAISI Sec. 102 (including 102(e), the $100M/yr FY2027-2029 authorization, plus fee authority); incident reporting Sec. 111(f)-(g); IVO audits Sec. 112; whistleblower Sec. 113; preemption Sec. 121; GAO report Sec. 122; fraud/impersonation Secs. 131-132; open-source security Sec. 311; testbeds Sec. 401; standards coalitions Sec. 411; NAIRR Sec. 423. Also verified: penalties up to $1M per day per violation, state AG opt-in to receive reports and audit findings, and CAISI's current appropriation of about $15M (per the sponsors' own FAQ). Draft will cite only what I checked."}
